#: ../../edge.rst:73
msgid ""
"The path of a connection through a node. Each box is owned by one thread "
"or one group of threads; the dashed edges are the shared, read-mostly "
"state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:109
msgid ""
"A QUIC client of an HTTP/3 frontend does not pass the edge worker: the "
"protocol runtime accepts it, applies the per-client bound through an "
"admission function, and asks the connection hooks when the ClientHello is "
"read (SID 0017, SID 0030)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:111
msgid "Readiness and the Cost of Waiting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:114
msgid ""
"The edge starts one HTTP worker for each processor the process may use, "
"up to 64. A worker is one thread with one poller (``epoll`` on Linux). "
"Every socket it owns is non-blocking, and the only wait of the thread is "
"the one call to ``epoll_wait`` in its loop. A worker is told which "
"sockets are *ready*: readable or writable now. It then does the work that "
"those bytes allow and returns to the loop. No thread ever waits for a "
"slow client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:116
msgid ""
"A connection is a record in the worker and a socket in the kernel. While "
"it is idle it holds no buffer. When its socket becomes readable the "
"worker reads into a block of its own and lends the connection a 64 KiB "
"buffer from its pool only while the connection holds unprocessed request "
"bytes. This is why waiting is cheap. In the acceptance tests of SID 0016, "
"20,000 idle keep-alive connections added 8 MiB of resident memory, and "
"10,000 open WebSockets held 27 MiB. SID 0015 puts an idle HTTP connection "
"at about 0.4 KiB in the process, against about 145 KiB on the thread "
"path. Kernel socket memory, about 3.4 KiB before any buffer, is then the "
"larger cost."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:118
msgid ""
"The firewall decision did not become asynchronous. It needs no I/O, so it "
"stays the synchronous function of the earlier Parts. The worker calls it "
"when a complete head, and the start of the body, are in memory. On "
"admission the decision returns a *plan*: the server reservation, the "
"encoded head, and the framing of the upload. Three sans-I/O machines then "
"carry the exchange: a request encoder, an upload relay and a response "
"relay. Each consumes bytes and produces bytes and never touches a socket. "
"Each is tested against the blocking relay it replaced: for 150,000 "
"mutated responses, fed whole, in random pieces and one byte at a time, "
"the bytes and the results are equal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:120
msgid "Every resource has a bound, and every state has a deadline:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:122
msgid ""
"The worker reads from a socket only while the buffer toward the other "
"side is below 64 KiB. A slow reader thereby stops the reads of its peer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:123
msgid ""
"One readiness event reads each socket of a connection at most once, at "
"most 64 KiB, and causes at most eight rounds of work. A bulk transfer "
"cannot starve the small requests of the same worker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:124
msgid ""
"A timer wheel with one-second slots holds one deadline per connection: "
"the idle timeout, a slow head, a slow body, a connect of five seconds, "
"server silence, a write that makes no progress."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:125
msgid ""
"A connection serves at most 4,096 requests. A worker keeps at most 64 "
"idle server sockets per address and 1,024 in all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:127
msgid ""
"With these workers, plaintext HTTP at 256 connections reached 229,139 "
"requests per second against HAProxy's 243,864, which is 94%, with a 99th "
"percentile of 2.60 ms (SID 0016)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:129
msgid "Implementation: ``Workers::submit``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:131
msgid ""
"Gives an accepted connection to the worker that serves the fewest "
"connections, raises that worker's count and wakes its poller. The "
"connection then stays on that worker. In "
"``cmd/sibuna/src/edge/workers.rs`` (crate ``sibuna``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:133
msgid "One Hop Fewer for HTTP/2 and HTTP/3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:136
msgid ""
"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 are terminated by a protocol runtime that uses "
"maintained libraries (rustls, hyper, quinn with h3). At first the runtime "
"wrote each request as HTTP/1.1 into a socket pair, and a worker read it, "
"parsed it again, decided it and forwarded it. SID 0017 measured an HTTP/2 "
"request at 76 us of CPU time: 46 us in the runtime before a worker saw "
"the request, and 30 us more in the worker to read the same request from a "
"socket and decide it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:138
msgid ""
"The repair is to call the decision as a function on the thread that "
"decoded the stream. The ingress crate defines plain data, ``Offer`` (the "
"head as HTTP/1.1 text and the start of the body) and ``Verdict`` (an "
"answer, or a ``Forward`` with the server reservation and the head to "
"send), and one trait, ``Decide``, which the binary implements with the "
"dispatch of the workers. An admitted request goes to its server on a "
"connection that the runtime pools itself. The request no longer crosses a "
"socket pair and a second thread. HTTP/2 throughput rose by 20% to 37% on "
"the same processors, and resident memory at 2,048 connections fell from "
"771 MiB to 592 MiB. Writing an HTTP/2 implementation of our own would "
"remove more overhead; SID 0017 declines it, because the defences against "
"rapid resets, CONTINUATION floods and HPACK expansion would then become "
"our own work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:140
msgid "TLS on the Workers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:143
msgid ""
"HTTPS kept one crossing: the runtime ended TLS and copied the clear bytes "
"to a worker through a socket pair. SID 0024 lets the worker end the TLS "
"of an HTTP/1.1 connection itself. The worker reads the ClientHello "
"without answering. If the client offers ``h2`` or ``acme-tls/1``, or "
"names a host whose certificate must first be obtained on demand, the "
"worker gives the socket and the bytes it read to the runtime. Otherwise "
"it keeps the connection and drives the TLS library without I/O of its "
"own. The worker decides from the offer, not from the outcome, because a "
"session that has answered cannot move to another thread. The work for a "
"client that has not finished its handshake is bounded by 16 KiB of first "
"message and 15 seconds. On the benchmark host, HTTPS at 256 connections "
"rose from 149,168 to 174,901 requests per second, where HAProxy writing "
"the same headers carries 153,080."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:147
msgid ""
"\"A worker never waits for a peer.\" Explain how this sentence, together "
"with the 64 KiB high-water mark, guarantees that a client which stops "
"reading its responses cannot make a worker hold an unbounded amount of "
"memory. Then name the one kind of call that still holds a worker for a "
"round trip (see the section on hooks)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:149
msgid "Choosing a Server"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:152
msgid ""
"A new flow first filters the servers of its pool. A server is *eligible* "
"when it is not the frontend's own address, not drained, healthy by its "
"last probe, below ``max_flows``, within its share of a fleet-wide limit, "
"not marked by a connect that failed in the last two seconds, and not "
"already tried by this request. A server ejected by passive health is "
"excluded only while some other server is eligible. In the wide-area "
"regime of a mesh, local servers are preferred when one is eligible. "
"Backups are chosen only when no primary server is eligible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:154
msgid ""
"Among the eligible servers the choice is made in a fixed order: a server "
"named by a sticky cookie or by the learned table, then the server that "
"source stickiness ranks first, then the Maglev primary when it is within "
"its load bound, and otherwise the pool's scheduler: round robin, power of "
"two choices, or weighted least connections. Every scheduler works with "
"integer weights in units of 1/256, so that slow start can lower a weight "
"in fine steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:156
msgid "Weighted Round Robin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:159
msgid ""
"Round robin is the smooth weighted round robin of nginx (SID 0019). Each "
"eligible server holds a credit. At each choice every server gains its "
"weight in credit, the server with the most credit is chosen, and it pays "
"the sum :math:`W` of the weights. Over :math:`W` choices the credits "
"return to where they started, and server :math:`i` was chosen exactly "
":math:`w_i` times."
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:161
msgid "Worked example: three servers of weights 5, 1 and 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../edge.rst:163
msgid ""
":math:`W = 7`. All credits start at 0. The table shows the credits after "
"the gain, the choice, and the chosen server's credit after it pays 7. "
"Ties go to the first server."
msgstr ""

